83 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 71 0 R
/E (The very often made, and very wrong, assumption that people have is that Artificial Intelligence is neutral. It is commonly assumed that AI is free of human biases, and that its judgements are totally free of prejudices. This is, however, not the case.)
/Pg 10 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
84 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 71 0 R
/E (The Red Apple Effect)
/Pg 10 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
85 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 71 0 R
/E (Artificial Intelligence is trained upon data created by humans. Texts, images, videos that humans have produced. The data that AI consumes is inherently filled with human bias. Specifically, these biases can be reflected in ways such as showing more favorable behavior to a certain group, whether that group may be categorized using race, gender or sexuality. )
/Pg 10 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
86 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 71 0 R
/E (There are many case studies that reflect this inherent bias trained into AI.)
/Pg 10 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
87 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 71 0 R
/E (The red apple effect is a phenomena observed in Artificial Intelligence, a sort of bias that is trained into the AI itself. The red apple effect is demonstrated through a thought experiment. An AI model is trained on photos of red apples, so that it can recognize apples. However, the AI mistakenly connects the color red with an apple, and therefore categorizing tomatos as apples and green apples as non-apples.)
/Pg 10 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
88 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 71 0 R
