>>
endobj
264 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 79 0 R
/E (This kind of selection bias is also present between age groups. Most studies done about sleep disorders were conducted using younger patients as test subjects, meaning that AI models that were trained on that data have a difficult time deciphering sleep disorders in older patients. )
/Pg 18 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
265 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 79 0 R
/E (These biases are further concerning in areas where the lack of variety is harder to detect such as drug development. When trials of new drugs are done on a group that is mainly focused on one ethnic group, a certain age range or gender can cause the features of the drug such as effects, side effects and effectiveness can transfer into AI models trained on this data. Creating bias that is harder to detect in the early stages of development.)
/Pg 18 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
266 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 79 0 R
/E (The lack of diversity in training data is only one flaw that results in biased AI models. Unconscious bias from medical professionals may make itself into research, judgement and evaluations. These biased standpoints can weave itself into AI models overtime, causing bias.)
/Pg 18 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
267 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 79 0 R
/E (The identification of such biases and efforts to remove them are crucial in maintaining a safe and fair healthcare system. There must a continuous search for gaps in the data, and investigations on which systems are biased. Research must be done to fill in these gaps and eliminate the systematic biases that AI models may have.)
/Pg 18 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
268 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 79 0 R
/E (A skin lesion is any part of the skin that differs from the tissue surrounding it. This could be a bump, redness, rashes or spots. Specific types of skin lesions are indications of more serious issues such as skin cancer, which means effective and quick identification of what these skin lesions are is extremely important.)
/Pg 18 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
269 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 79 0 R
/E (Skin lesion identification softwares are trained on mostly white skin, with only 5~10% of training subjects being black. Therefore, when it comes to identifying skin lesions of black individuals, the accuracy rate decreases 50% compared to when identifying skin lesions on white skin. This issue is even more concerning considering that black people have a higher fatality rate due to melanoma, a dangerous type of skin cancer, compared to white patient. There is a 70% 5 year survival rate for black patients, which is significantly lower than the 90% of white individuals.)
/Pg 18 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
